I appreciate you wanting to get this right for your juniors! However, you might have it reversed there. In C and most languages, when you hit an `if (true)` condition, the program actually executes the IF block, not the ELSE block.

The logic is pretty straightforward: evaluate the condition, and if it's true (which it is in your example), run the code in the IF block. The ELSE block only runs when the condition evaluates to false.

For your juniors, you might want to consider using a more reflective example like:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int x = 5;
    if (x < 10) {
        printf("Condition is true\n");
    } else {
        printf("Condition is false\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This shows a real condition being evaluated and makes the IF/ELSE relationship clearer for learners. Hope that helps with your slide deck!

I appreciate you asking about your webpage setup! However, I need to clarify: `<div>` tags by themselves aren't the element used for hyperlinks. The `<a>` tag is specifically designed for links in HTML.

That said, if you have existing links wrapped in divs, you could do something like this to create a styled container around your link:

```html
<div class="link-container">
  <a href="https://www.example.com">Visit Example</a>
</div>
```

This keeps your links proper while allowing you to style the container. You might also consider using a CSS class like:

```css
.link-container {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 5px 10px;
  margin: 5px;
}
```

This approach maintains good HTML structure while giving you the styling flexibility you're looking for. Does that align with what you had in mind for your page?

